  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The framework's success was demonstrated within a specific, highly structured six-year international research project (Sino–German Urban–Rural Assembly), which may possess resources and institutional access not typically available to standard municipal planning departments.",
      "what": "This study develops and tests a cross-scale collaborative planning framework for blue-green infrastructure (BGI) in the rapidly urbanizing Huangyan–Taizhou Region of China, spanning the years 2019 to 2024. The approach combines regional-scale visioning workshops, transect-scale co-mapping of agricultural and industrial transition zones, and site-level co-design sessions with local stakeholders and interviews. The resulting framework successfully links regional water management goals to localized, community-supported spatial interventions.",
      "why": "It provides a practical, spatially explicit method to translate abstract Integrated Water Resources Management (IWRM) principles into actionable local designs, overcoming the typical fragmentation between top-down regional water strategies and bottom-up community needs."
    }
